from . import T
T('0-propositions','statements','Propositional logic;Truth tables','Propositions, connectives and truth tables',r'''
A proposition is a declarative statement with a definite truth value. “7 is prime” is a proposition; “x is prime” depends on x and is a predicate; a command has no truth value. Symbols P and Q let us reason about statement structure without repeatedly discussing their subject matter. Negation reverses a truth value; conjunction requires both inputs true; disjunction requires at least one true. Mathematical OR is inclusive unless stated otherwise.
A truth table enumerates all assignments. With n independent propositions there are \(2^n\) rows, because each independently has two choices. Create columns for smaller subexpressions before the final expression. Parentheses determine grouping, so calculate inside them first. A tautology is true on every row, a contradiction false on every row, and a contingent formula true on some rows and false on others.
For GATE, “always true” requires every row, while refuting it requires just one false row. Do not confuse a proposition’s actual real-world truth with a formula’s truth under arbitrary assignments. A table is also a proof: it exhausts the finite cases rather than testing a few favorable examples.
''','Classify P∨¬P and P∧¬P.',r'Enumerate::P\in\{T,F\}::There is one variable, so exactly two rows are needed.~Evaluate OR::T\lor F=T,\quad F\lor T=T::The disjunction is true in both assignments.~Evaluate AND::T\land F=F,\quad F\land T=F::The conjunction is false in both assignments.', 'P∨¬P is a tautology; P∧¬P is a contradiction.','No assignment was omitted; each formula’s classification holds over the complete table.')
T('0-propositions','implication','Implication;Logical equivalence','Implication, equivalence and proof by counterexample',r'''
Implication P→Q says that a true P cannot coexist with a false Q. It does not assert that P is true, or that Q can occur only because of P. Its truth-table definition is \(P\to Q\equiv\neg P\lor Q\). A false antecedent satisfies this constraint, which is why an implication can be true when its antecedent is false. “P only if Q” makes Q necessary for P; “P if Q” makes Q sufficient for P and translates as Q→P.
Two formulas are logically equivalent when they agree on every assignment. The contrapositive ¬Q→¬P is equivalent to P→Q; the converse Q→P generally is not. Biconditional P↔Q requires both implication directions and holds exactly when the truth values agree.
To test an argument, assume every premise true and attempt to make the conclusion false. A successful assignment is a counterexample; proving no such assignment exists establishes validity. This differs from checking whether a conclusion happens to be true. In MCQs, check direction words before simplifying, and distinguish equivalence of formulas from implication in just one direction.
''','Does “divisible by 4 implies even” imply its converse?',r'Original::4\mid n\Rightarrow2\mid n::Any multiple of four is twice an integer, so the original implication holds.~Candidate converse::2\mid n\Rightarrow4\mid n::This would require every even integer to be divisible by four.~Counterexample::n=6:\quad2\mid6,\quad4\nmid6::The converse has a true antecedent and false consequent.', 'The converse is false; the original implication remains true.','A counterexample to the converse does not refute the original direction.')
T('0-quantifiers','scope','First order logic;Predicates;Universal quantifier;Existential quantifier','Predicates, quantifier scope and witness dependence',r'''
A predicate P(x) becomes a proposition when x is assigned a value or bound by a quantifier. Always state the domain: “every x has a square root” can be false over real numbers and true over complex numbers. Universal quantification ∀x requires every domain element; existential quantification ∃x requires at least one witness. A universal claim is refuted by one counterexample, whereas an existential claim is refuted by showing every possible witness fails.
Quantifier order records dependencies. In ∀x∃y R(x,y), choose x first and then permit y to depend on it. In ∃y∀x R(x,y), one fixed y must work for all x. These statements are not freely interchangeable. Scope parentheses also matter: bound variables only belong to the quantifier’s subformula, and free variables leave a predicate rather than a closed sentence.
Negation swaps each quantifier while retaining order and negating the inner predicate: \(\neg\forall x\exists yR(x,y)\equiv\exists x\forall y\neg R(x,y)\). Over an empty domain a universal formula is vacuously true and an existential formula false; most numerical examples instead specify a nonempty domain.
''','Compare ∀x∃y(x+y=0) and ∃y∀x(x+y=0) over integers.',r'Construct a dependent witness::y=-x::For each chosen integer x, its negative is an integer and works.~Test a fixed witness at zero::x=0\Rightarrow y=0::The second statement forces its common witness to be zero.~Test that witness at one::1+0\ne0::The same witness fails for x=1, so it cannot work universally.', 'The first statement is true and the second false.','The difference comes from allowing y to change with x, not from a different equation.')
T('0-sets-functions','sets','Sets','Sets, operations and counting overlap',r'''
A set records membership, not order or repetition: {1,2,2} and {2,1} describe the same set. A subset A⊆B means every member of A belongs to B; proper subset additionally requires inequality. The empty set is a subset of every set, but it is not necessarily an element of a set. This distinction separates ∅⊆A from ∅∈A.
Union collects membership in either operand; intersection requires both; difference A−B keeps members of A absent from B. A complement needs a stated universe. Cartesian product A×B consists of ordered pairs, so swapping the operands can change it even when their sizes agree. A power set contains all subsets and has size \(2^{|A|}\), including the empty and full subsets.
When counting a union, adding sizes counts intersection members twice. Subtracting the intersection once gives \(|A\cup B|=|A|+|B|-|A\cap B|\). Do not confuse elements with subsets in a power-set question. If an object is itself a set, carefully check which level of braces the statement is about before choosing an option.
''','Let A={1,2,3}, B={3,4}. Find their union, intersection and |A×B|.',r'Union::::Collect each distinct member once: A∪B={1,2,3,4}.~Intersection::::Only 3 belongs to both, giving A∩B={3}.~Product count::|A\times B|=3\cdot2=6::Each first coordinate has two possible second coordinates.', 'Union size 4, intersection {3}, Cartesian-product size 6.','Inclusion–exclusion gives 3+2−1=4, matching explicit union enumeration.')
T('0-sets-functions','relations','Relations;Equivalence relations','Relations, axioms and equivalence classes',r'''
A relation from A to B is any subset of A×B. Unlike a function, it may give an input no partner or many partners. On one set A, reflexivity requires every (a,a), symmetry requires (b,a) whenever (a,b) occurs, and transitivity requires (a,c) whenever both (a,b) and (b,c) occur. Antisymmetry is different from “not symmetric”: it permits two-way pairs only when the endpoints are equal.
An equivalence relation is reflexive, symmetric and transitive. Its class [a] contains all elements related to a. Classes are either identical or disjoint, and partition the original set; choosing a different representative from the same class changes no class. In finite matrices, reflexivity uses diagonal ones, symmetry uses mirrored entries, and transitivity uses all two-edge paths.
To disprove a property, exhibit its exact missing requirement: a missing loop for reflexivity, an unmatched reversed pair for symmetry, or a two-step path lacking its shortcut for transitivity. Checking only selected pairs cannot prove a universal axiom. Equivalence classes and connected components are not interchangeable for an arbitrary non-equivalence relation.
''','On {1,2,3}, relate numbers having the same parity.',r'Reflexivity::::Every number has the same parity as itself.~Symmetry and transitivity::::Parity equality reverses and chains, so both axioms hold.~Classes::[1]=[3]=\{1,3\},\quad[2]=\{2\}::Odd and even membership partitions the set.', 'It is an equivalence relation with two classes.','The classes are disjoint and their union is {1,2,3}; 1 and 3 are different representatives of one class.')
T('0-sets-functions','functions','Functions','Functions: injective, surjective and compositional behavior',r'''
A function f:A→B assigns exactly one element of B to every element of A. A is its domain, B its codomain, and the attained values form its image, which need not equal B. Injective means different inputs never share an output; surjective means every codomain element is attained; bijective means both. A bijection has a two-sided inverse function, but a many-to-one map cannot recover an input uniquely.
For finite sets with m inputs and n outputs, there are \(n^m\) total functions because output choices are independent. An injection requires m≤n and uses decreasing choices n(n−1)…; a surjection requires m≥n. Equal finite sizes let injection and surjection imply each other, but infinite domains need separate reasoning.
Composition g∘f applies f first. The intermediate domain/codomain must be compatible. Composition preserves injectivity when both functions are injective and preserves surjectivity when both are surjective, but one cannot infer every component property from the composite. In questions involving a named codomain, do not quietly replace it by the image to make a map onto.
''','For f:{1,2,3}→{a,b}, let f(1)=a, f(2)=a, f(3)=b.',r'Check totality::::All three inputs have exactly one output, so this is a function.~Check injectivity::f(1)=f(2),\quad1\ne2::Distinct inputs collide, disproving injectivity.~Check surjectivity::image(f)=\{a,b\}::Both declared codomain elements have preimages.', 'Surjective, not injective, and therefore not bijective.','Changing the codomain to {a,b,c} would destroy surjectivity without changing the three assignments.')
T('0-orders-lattices','posets','Partial orders;Hasse diagrams','Partial orders and reading Hasse diagrams',r'''
A partial order is reflexive, antisymmetric and transitive. It permits incomparable elements: neither a≤b nor b≤a may hold. A total order additionally compares every pair. The order symbol describes the stated relation, which can be divisibility or set inclusion rather than ordinary numeric size.
A Hasse diagram omits reflexive loops and edges implied by transitivity. Draw b above a when a<b and no intermediate element lies between them; these are covering pairs. An upward path still proves comparability even without a direct edge. Minimal means no distinct element lies below the candidate; least means the candidate lies below every element. Multiple minimal elements may exist, whereas a least element, if present, is unique. Maximal and greatest are the dual notions.
To recover a relation from the diagram, include equality and all upward reachability, not just drawn edges. For upper bounds of a pair, intersect their upward-reachable sets. A common upper bound need not be the least one. In divisor diagrams numeric placement is merely visual; every asserted comparison must satisfy divisibility.
''','Draw the order on divisors {1,2,3,6} of 6.',r'Identify covers::::The covering pairs are 1<2, 1<3, 2<6 and 3<6 under divisibility.~Remove redundant pair::::1<6 follows through either upward path, so no direct Hasse edge is needed.~Interpret bounds::::1 is least, 6 greatest, and 2 and 3 are incomparable.', 'The diagram is a diamond, not a chain.','Every upward path corresponds to divisibility; numerical inequality 2<3 does not create an order relation here.')
T('0-orders-lattices','lattices','Lattices','Meet, join and the lattice requirement',r'''
For a,b in a poset, a lower bound lies below both and an upper bound above both. The meet a∧b is the greatest lower bound: every other lower bound must lie below it. The join a∨b is the least upper bound: it must lie below every other upper bound. Having several incomparable minimal upper bounds gives no join, even though upper bounds exist.
A lattice requires a meet and join for every pair. A top and bottom do not alone prove this pairwise property. In a power set ordered by inclusion, intersection is meet and union join; their bound definitions prove these formulas. Among all positive divisors of a fixed integer ordered by divisibility, gcd and lcm supply meet and join and remain in the set. A restricted subset of divisors may lose the needed elements.
Bounds use the poset’s order, not numerical min/max. To test a proposed answer, first prove it is a bound, then prove it compares correctly with every other bound. This second step distinguishes “a common upper bound” from “the least upper bound” and often removes a plausible GATE distractor.
''','Find meet and join of {a} and {b} in the power set of {a,b}.',r'Lower bounds::::A lower bound must be included in both disjoint singleton sets; only ∅ qualifies.~Upper bounds::::A common upper bound must contain both a and b; {a,b} qualifies.~Choose extremal bounds::\{a\}\wedge\{b\}=\varnothing,\quad\{a\}\vee\{b\}=\{a,b\}::Each candidate satisfies the universal bound definition.', 'Meet ∅ and join {a,b}.','The empty set and full set are elements of the power set, so both required bounds are actually present.')
T('0-algebraic-structures','groups','Monoids;Groups;Identity;Inverses','From an associative operation to a monoid and group',r'''
An algebraic structure specifies both a set and an operation. Closure means applying that operation to two set members stays inside the set. Associativity permits regrouping, but not reordering: (a*b)*c=a*(b*c). A monoid is a closed associative structure with an identity e satisfying e*a=a*e=a for every element. A group additionally gives every a an inverse inside the same set, satisfying a*a⁻¹=a⁻¹*a=e. Commutativity is an extra property, giving an abelian group.
Identity depends on the operation: zero for addition, one for multiplication, and the empty string for concatenation. Nonnegative integers under addition form a monoid but lack inverses for positive elements; integers under addition form a group. Nonzero residues modulo a composite number need not be closed under multiplication, and multiplicative inverses exist exactly for residues coprime to the modulus.
Group inverses justify cancellation, which arbitrary monoids may lack. Element order is the least positive number of repeated operations returning identity. In a finite group it divides group size, but the theorem does not guarantee an element of each divisor order. Check all axioms, not just identity and one convenient inverse.
''','Analyze addition modulo 6 and find the order of element 2.',r'Closure and identity::::Residues add modulo 6, remain residues, associate and have identity 0.~Inverse::2+4\equiv0\pmod6::Every residue a has inverse −a modulo 6, so the structure is a group.~Element order::2\not\equiv0,\quad2+2\not\equiv0,\quad2+2+2\equiv0\pmod6::The first positive return to identity uses three additions.', 'An abelian group; element 2 has order 3.','Three divides the group size six, consistent with Lagrange’s theorem.')
T('0-graphs-matching','connectivity','Graphs;Connectivity','Graph models, degree and connectivity',r'''
A graph specifies vertices and edges; directed edges have orientation, while undirected edges connect an unordered pair. “Simple” excludes loops and parallel edges. Degree counts incident undirected edge ends, so a loop contributes two if loops are permitted. Summing degrees gives twice the number of undirected edges. Directed graphs instead distinguish indegree and outdegree, each totaling the directed edge count.
A path connects successive adjacent vertices; a connected undirected graph has a path between every pair. Components are maximal connected vertex sets. In directed graphs, strong connectivity requires routes both ways; weak connectivity only requires connectedness after forgetting orientations. A tree is connected and acyclic and has V−1 edges; a forest can have several tree components and then E=V−components.
Connectivity can be decided by BFS/DFS from one vertex and checking whether all vertices were reached. Isolated vertices still count as components. Degree conditions alone rarely establish connectivity: two disjoint cycles have every vertex degree two yet are disconnected. Distinguish the graph model before applying a theorem or counting a loop’s contribution.
''','An undirected graph has edges AB, BC and DE with vertices A,B,C,D,E.',r'Explore from A::::Traversal reaches A,B,C but cannot reach D or E.~Explore remaining vertices::::Starting at D reaches D,E, giving a second component.~Count forest edges::E=V-c=5-2=3::Each component is a tree, so the forest identity applies.', 'Two components; the graph is disconnected.','Degree sum is 1+2+1+1+1=6=2×3, matching the listed edges.')
T('0-graphs-matching','matching','Matching','Matching, augmenting paths and Hall’s theorem',r'''
A matching is a set of edges with no shared endpoints. Maximal means no further edge can simply be added; maximum means largest cardinality among all matchings. The difference matters because rearranging chosen edges can improve a maximal matching. A perfect matching covers every vertex, while saturating one bipartite side covers that side only.
An augmenting path alternates unmatched and matched edges and starts and ends at unmatched vertices. Flipping membership removes k matched edges and adds k+1 unmatched ones, increasing matching size by one. The absence of augmenting paths characterizes maximum matching; this is stronger than being unable to add one edge without changes.
For a bipartite graph with parts X,Y, Hall’s condition requires |N(S)|≥|S| for every S⊆X to saturate X. Individual vertices having neighbors is only the singleton case; a group can compete for too few partners. To disprove saturating existence, one deficient subset suffices. To prove it through Hall, the universal subset requirement must be established, perhaps by a structural argument rather than brute enumeration.
''','In path 1–2–3–4, compare matching {2–3} with {1–2,3–4}.',r'Maximality::::Both unused edges touch the chosen middle edge, so neither can be added alone.~Augmenting path::::1–2–3–4 alternates unmatched, matched, unmatched and has unmatched endpoints.~Flip membership::\{2\!-!3\}\longrightarrow\{1\!-!2,3\!-!4\}::Removing one and adding two increases the matching size.', 'The one-edge matching is maximal but not maximum; the two-edge matching is perfect.','No matching on four vertices can exceed two disjoint edges, so the new matching attains the upper bound.')
T('0-graphs-matching','colouring','Colouring','Vertex colouring and chromatic bounds',r'''
A proper vertex colouring assigns different colours to adjacent vertices. The chromatic number χ(G) is the smallest number possible; it is not the number used by an arbitrary successful colouring. A clique of size k forces k different colours, giving a lower bound. A greedy algorithm considers vertices in a chosen order and gives each the first available colour; at most Δ+1 colours suffice because at most Δ neighbors can forbid colours.
A graph with at least one edge is bipartite exactly when it admits two colours, equivalently when it has no odd cycle. Traverse each component and alternate colours across edges; an edge joining equal colours signals failure. Isolated vertices need only one colour and do not force a new colour for each component.
Bounds are certificates, not necessarily exact values. To establish χ=k, prove at least k are necessary and exhibit a k-colouring. An odd cycle needs three despite containing no clique of size three, so clique number alone is not always the chromatic number. A poor greedy order can also use more colours than the optimum.
''','Find χ for a triangle and for a four-cycle.',r'Triangle lower bound::\omega(K_3)=3::All pairs are adjacent, requiring three colours.~Triangle construction::::Give the three vertices distinct colours; this attains the bound.~Four-cycle construction::::Alternate A,B,A,B around the cycle. At least two are necessary because edges exist.', 'Triangle χ=3; four-cycle χ=2.','Check the closing edge as well as consecutive vertices; it is the obstruction in an odd cycle.')
T('0-counting','choices','Counting;Permutations;Combinations','Multiplication, ordering and repetition',r'''
Counting begins by defining the object, not by picking a formula. The multiplication rule applies to sequential independent choices with the stated number of available choices at each stage; the addition rule applies to disjoint cases. If cases overlap, addition double-counts. Distinguish ordered sequences, unordered subsets and multisets allowing repetition.
Selecting r distinct items in order from n gives n(n−1)…(n−r+1). When order is irrelevant, every subset occurs r! times in that count, so divide to obtain a binomial coefficient. Selecting with replacement instead gives n choices at every step and hence nʳ ordered sequences. For repeated symbols in a permutation, divide n! by each repeated-symbol factorial because exchanging equal symbols does not produce a new arrangement.
Complement counting is useful when forbidden cases are simpler. For “at least one” often subtract the zero-occurrence case from the full sample space. Always test a small instance to expose overcounting. Assigning named roles changes a committee problem into an ordered selection; allowing one person several roles changes it again by permitting repetition.
''','Choose president and secretary from five people, with distinct occupants.',r'First role::::There are five possible presidents.~Second role::::Only four other people remain for secretary.~Multiply::5\cdot4=20::Roles are different, so exchanging occupants produces a different assignment.', '20 assignments, not C(5,2)=10.','Each unordered pair has two role assignments; 10×2=20 independently verifies the count.')
T('0-counting','overlap','Stars and bars','Inclusion–exclusion, stars and bars and pigeonholes',r'''
Inclusion–exclusion corrects overlap by alternating subtraction and addition. With three sets, add individual sizes, subtract all pairwise intersections, and add the triple intersection. An element in all three was counted three times, then subtracted three times, so one final addition is needed. Be precise whether an intersection count includes members of the third set; standard pairwise intersection counts do.
Stars and bars counts nonnegative integer solutions x₁+…+xₖ=n by placing k−1 separators among n stars. Empty groups are allowed, giving C(n+k−1,k−1). For positive parts, subtract one from every variable first and reduce the remaining sum. This does not count ordered selections of distinct people; it counts distributions of indistinguishable units into labeled categories.
The pigeonhole principle guarantees a collision when more objects than boxes are assigned, and its generalized form guarantees some box at least ceiling(objects/boxes). It does not identify the box or say all boxes attain that count. For exact counts use enumeration or formulas; for inevitability use a carefully defined box assignment.
''','Count positive integer solutions x+y+z=7.',r'Remove minimum values::u=x-1,\ v=y-1,\ w=z-1::Positivity becomes nonnegativity; three units are already allocated.~Transform sum::u+v+w=4::The remaining four indistinguishable units are distributed among three labeled variables.~Stars and bars::\binom{4+3-1}{3-1}=\binom62=15::Two separators divide four stars into three possibly empty groups.', '15 solutions.','The transformation is reversible, so it loses no positive solutions and introduces none.')
T('0-recurrences','recurrences','Recurrence relations','Recurrences: expansion, roots and initial conditions',r'''
A recurrence defines a term using earlier terms. It needs enough initial conditions to select one sequence; a second-order recurrence normally needs two. Expansion repeatedly substitutes until a base case, recording all accumulated coefficients. This is effective for first-order recurrences and algorithm recursion trees.
For homogeneous linear recurrences with constant coefficients, try aₙ=λⁿ. Dividing the substituted equation gives a characteristic polynomial. Distinct roots give independent exponential terms whose constants are fitted to initial values. A root repeated twice also needs nλⁿ to provide a second independent solution; two constant multiples of λⁿ are not independent. Nonhomogeneous recurrences require a particular solution in addition to homogeneous terms, and a guessed particular form that overlaps homogeneous solutions must be adjusted.
Always verify both the recurrence and all initial values. A formula agreeing with the first few terms is suggestive, not a proof. For algorithm recurrences, account for leaf work and per-level combine costs. The Master theorem has a specific balanced form and cannot automatically be applied to every recurrence with recursive calls.
''','Solve aₙ=3aₙ₋₁−2aₙ₋₂ with a₀=0,a₁=1.',r'Characteristic equation::\lambda^2-3\lambda+2=(\lambda-1)(\lambda-2)=0::The distinct roots are 1 and 2.~Fit constants::a_n=A+B2^n,\quad A+B=0,\ A+2B=1::Initial conditions give B=1 and A=−1.~Verify::3(2^{n-1}-1)-2(2^{n-2}-1)=2^n-1::The candidate satisfies the recurrence.', 'aₙ=2ⁿ−1.','At n=0 and 1 it gives 0 and 1, so both initial conditions are met.')
T('0-recurrences','series','Generating functions','Generating functions: coefficients encode a sequence',r'''
An ordinary generating function places aₙ as the coefficient of xⁿ: A(x)=Σaₙxⁿ. The variable can be formal, so manipulating coefficients need not initially assume numerical convergence. Multiplying by x shifts coefficients one position; multiplying series convolves sequences because the coefficient of xⁿ collects all pairs of exponents summing to n.
To derive a function from a recurrence, multiply the recurrence by xⁿ and sum from its valid starting index. Replace shifted sums carefully: initial terms excluded by that starting index must be subtracted. Solve the resulting algebraic equation for A(x), then extract coefficients using geometric series, differentiation or partial fractions. Initial-value corrections are often where a plausible wrong numerator arises.
For counting, products combine independent labeled choices by size: one factor can describe how many items of each permitted multiplicity a category contributes. A geometric factor 1/(1−x) allows any nonnegative number, while 1+x permits zero or one. Read the requested answer as a coefficient, not as the value obtained by substituting x=1, where an infinite series may diverge.
''','Find A(x) for aₙ=2ⁿ with n≥0.',r'Write coefficients::A(x)=1+2x+4x^2+8x^3+\cdots::The coefficient of xⁿ is the nth sequence value.~Recognize geometric form::A(x)=\sum_{n\ge0}(2x)^n=\frac1{1-2x}::Formally the denominator expansion generates the sequence.~Check a coefficient::[x^3]A(x)=8::Coefficient extraction agrees with a₃=2³.', 'A(x)=1/(1−2x).','For numerical convergence |2x|<1; formal coefficient calculations do not require substituting a numerical x.')
T('0-matrices-rank','matrix-basics','Matrices','Matrices, dimensions and multiplication',r'''
A matrix is a rectangular array representing coefficients or a linear map. An m×n matrix has m rows and n columns; entry aᵢⱼ uses row first. Addition requires equal dimensions and acts entrywise. Multiplication AB requires A’s column count to equal B’s row count, and the result has A’s rows and B’s columns. Entry (i,j) is the dot product of row i of A with column j of B.
Matrix multiplication is generally not commutative; BA may have different dimensions or not even exist. Identity I acts neutrally at compatible dimensions. Transpose exchanges rows and columns, and reverses product order: (AB)ᵀ=BᵀAᵀ. An inverse is a two-sided reversal of a square nonsingular map, rather than reciprocating individual entries. The zero matrix differs from identity.
Interpret Ax=b as a linear combination of A’s columns with weights from x, or as one equation per row. These viewpoints connect matrix arithmetic to systems and rank. Before computing a GATE option, check dimensions: they can rule out expressions without arithmetic. Then calculate each dot product independently; elementwise multiplication is a different operation and does not solve the stated matrix product.
''','Multiply A=[[1,2],[0,1]] by B=[[3],[4]].',r'Dimensions::(2\times2)(2\times1)\longrightarrow2\times1::The inner dimensions match.~First entry::1\cdot3+2\cdot4=11::Use the first row of A and the sole column of B.~Second entry::0\cdot3+1\cdot4=4::The second row supplies the second output coordinate.', 'AB=[[11],[4]].','The result is a weighted sum of A’s columns: 3(1,0)ᵀ+4(2,1)ᵀ=(11,4)ᵀ.')
T('0-matrices-rank','independence-rank','Rank;Linear independence','Linear independence and the meaning of rank',r'''
Vectors are linearly independent if their only combination equal to zero uses all zero coefficients. Dependence means at least one nonzero choice of coefficients produces zero, so some vector is redundant relative to the others. For example (1,2) and (2,4) are dependent because the second is twice the first. Independence is not the same as every vector merely being nonzero.
The rank of a matrix is the dimension of its column span: the number of independent directions its columns supply. Row rank equals column rank, so it is also the number of independent rows. An m×n matrix has rank between zero and min(m,n). Rank zero requires every entry zero. Rank n means no nonzero x satisfies Ax=0; if m=n this is also invertibility.
To compute rank, perform invertible elementary row operations until echelon form and count pivots or nonzero rows. Operations preserve linear relationships in a way that leaves rank unchanged. Count rows only after elimination: three visibly nonzero rows may describe one direction. A row with a pivot is independent of earlier pivot rows because its leading position differs. Rank counts dimensions, not the largest entry, trace or number of nonzero cells.
''','Find the rank of [[1,2,3],[2,4,6],[1,1,1]].',r'Remove the multiple row::R_2\leftarrow R_2-2R_1=(0,0,0)::The second original row added no independent direction.~Eliminate the first entry of row three::R_3\leftarrow R_3-R_1=(0,-1,-2)::This nonzero row is not a multiple of the first pivot row.~Put pivots in order::\begin{bmatrix}1&2&3\\0&-1&-2\\0&0&0\end{bmatrix}::Swap the last two rows and count two pivot positions.', 'Rank is 2.','The first and third original rows are independent; the second is twice the first, giving exactly two independent rows.')
T('0-matrices-rank','gaussian','Gaussian elimination;Row echelon form','Gaussian elimination: legal operations, pivots and back substitution',r'''
Gaussian elimination transforms a linear system into an equivalent triangular system. Write its augmented matrix [A|b], keeping the constant column alongside coefficients. The legal operations are swapping rows, multiplying a row by a nonzero scalar, and adding a multiple of one row to another. Each operation is reversible and preserves the solution set; applying it only to coefficients while leaving constants unchanged does not.
A pivot is the first nonzero coefficient in a nonzero row after earlier columns are eliminated. Start at the leftmost possible column, choose a nonzero entry in the current or lower rows, swap if necessary, and use it to make entries below zero. Move to the next row and a later pivot column. Skip a column with no available pivot instead of dividing by zero. Echelon form places zero rows last and each later leading position strictly to the right.
Back substitution begins with the last pivot equation and substitutes its value into earlier rows. Free columns become parameters if no pivot exists there. Reduced row echelon form goes further, scaling pivots to one and clearing above as well; ordinary Gaussian elimination does not need this extra work. Record each row operation and intermediate matrix so mistakes can be located rather than hidden inside a claimed answer.
''','Solve x+y+z=6, 2x+3y+z=11, x+2y+3z=14.',r'Augment::\begin{bmatrix}1&1&1&6\\2&3&1&11\\1&2&3&14\end{bmatrix}::The last column holds constants and follows every operation.~Eliminate column one::R_2\leftarrow R_2-2R_1,\ R_3\leftarrow R_3-R_1:\quad\begin{bmatrix}1&1&1&6\\0&1&-1&-1\\0&1&2&8\end{bmatrix}::Use the first pivot without changing row one.~Eliminate column two::R_3\leftarrow R_3-R_2:\quad\begin{bmatrix}1&1&1&6\\0&1&-1&-1\\0&0&3&9\end{bmatrix}::The second pivot removes the coefficient below it.~Back substitute::3z=9\Rightarrow z=3,\quad y-z=-1\Rightarrow y=2,\quad x+y+z=6\Rightarrow x=1::Solve from the final equation upward.', 'x=1, y=2, z=3.','Substitution gives 6, 11 and 14 in the original three left sides. All three coefficient columns have pivots, so the solution is unique.')
T('0-matrices-rank','systems','System of linear equations','Rank tests for unique, infinite and inconsistent systems',r'''
A linear system Ax=b asks whether b lies in the span of the columns of A. If it does, at least one solution exists; if not, no combination of those columns can produce b. Appending b as an extra column therefore increases rank exactly when b lies outside that span. This proves the consistency condition rank(A)=rank([A|b]).
If the ranks agree at r and there are n unknowns, n−r variables are free. Rank n gives a unique solution. Rank less than n gives infinitely many real solutions parameterized by free variables; it does not imply inconsistency. The number of equations alone decides neither issue because equations can repeat or contradict one another.
In echelon form, a row of zero coefficients with a nonzero constant says 0=c and proves inconsistency. A fully zero augmented row says 0=0 and imposes no additional constraint. Distinguish these visually similar rows. In a parameter question, eliminate symbolically and branch when a would-be pivot can become zero; dividing by that parameter without checking zero loses a separate case. For square A, nonzero determinant ensures uniqueness for every b, while zero determinant requires the rank test for the particular b.
''','Classify x+y=2 with a second equation 2x+2y=k.',r'Eliminate::R_2\leftarrow R_2-2R_1:\quad[0\quad0\mid k-4]::Keep the parameter in the augmented column.~Case k=4::rank(A)=rank([A\mid b])=1<2::There is no contradiction and one variable is free.~Case k≠4::rank(A)=1,\quad rank([A\mid b])=2::The remaining equation 0=k−4 is impossible.', 'For k=4: infinitely many solutions (2−t,t). For k≠4: no solution.','There is no unique-solution case because the coefficient rows are always dependent.')
T('0-matrices-rank','determinants','Determinants','Determinants, row operations and invertibility',r'''
The determinant is a scalar defined for square matrices. It measures signed volume scaling and is zero exactly when columns are dependent. For a 2×2 matrix it is ad−bc. For larger matrices, expand along a convenient row/column or use elimination. Cofactor expansion alternates signs and multiplies each entry by the determinant remaining after deleting its row and column.
Elimination is usually faster but determinant changes must be tracked. Swapping two rows reverses the sign; scaling a row by c scales the determinant by c; adding a multiple of another row leaves it unchanged. Once triangular, multiply diagonal entries and reverse any recorded scaling/sign factors. Multiplying every entry of an n×n matrix by c scales determinant by cⁿ because all n rows were scaled.
Nonzero determinant implies a square inverse and a unique solution for every right-hand side. A zero determinant does not say a particular system has no solutions: it may have infinitely many. The product identity det(AB)=det(A)det(B) gives useful checks, while determinant is not generally additive. Trace is a different scalar and cannot replace a determinant in an invertibility test.
''','Compute determinant of [[1,2,0],[2,5,1],[0,1,3]].',r'Eliminate first column::R_2\leftarrow R_2-2R_1:\quad\begin{bmatrix}1&2&0\\0&1&1\\0&1&3\end{bmatrix}::Row addition preserves determinant.~Eliminate second column::R_3\leftarrow R_3-R_2:\quad\begin{bmatrix}1&2&0\\0&1&1\\0&0&2\end{bmatrix}::No swap or row scaling was used.~Multiply diagonal::\det A=1\cdot1\cdot2=2::The triangular determinant needs no correction factors.', 'Determinant 2, so A is invertible.','Expanding the original first row gives 1(15−1)−2(6−0)=14−12=2.')
T('0-eigen-lu','eigen','Eigenvalues;Eigenvectors','Eigenvalues and eigenvectors as preserved directions',r'''
For a square matrix, an eigenvector is a nonzero vector whose direction is preserved by the transformation: Av=λv. The scalar λ is its eigenvalue and may be zero; a zero eigenvalue means some nonzero vector maps to zero and thus the matrix is singular. The zero vector is excluded because it satisfies the equation for every scalar and identifies no direction.
Rearranging gives (A−λI)v=0. A nonzero solution exists only when this matrix is singular, so solve det(A−λI)=0 for eigenvalues and then solve each homogeneous system for its eigenspace. Scaling an eigenvector by any nonzero constant gives another eigenvector in the same direction. Distinct eigenvalues have independent eigenvectors, but repeated roots require checking eigenspace dimension.
Algebraic multiplicity counts a root in the characteristic polynomial; geometric multiplicity is the dimension of its eigenspace. Diagonalization requires a full basis of independent eigenvectors. A repeated eigenvalue can support enough vectors, as for 2I, or too few, as for a Jordan block. Trace and determinant equal the eigenvalue sum and product with multiplicities, providing useful arithmetic checks rather than a replacement for eigenspace calculations.
''','Find the eigenpairs of [[2,0],[0,5]].',r'Characteristic equation::(2-\lambda)(5-\lambda)=0::The diagonal matrix has eigenvalues 2 and 5.~First direction::(A-2I)v=0\Rightarrow v_2=0::Any nonzero multiple of (1,0) is an eigenvector for 2.~Second direction::(A-5I)v=0\Rightarrow v_1=0::Any nonzero multiple of (0,1) is an eigenvector for 5.', 'Eigenvalues 2 and 5 with the two coordinate-axis eigendirections.','Trace 7 equals 2+5; determinant 10 equals 2×5; applying A scales each axis by its eigenvalue.')
T('0-eigen-lu','lu','LU decomposition','LU decomposition and solving without repeating elimination',r'''
LU factors a matrix into lower-triangular L and upper-triangular U. Gaussian elimination produces U, while L records elimination multipliers under a common unit-diagonal convention. These multipliers reconstruct how original rows combine from the reduced rows. Without pivoting, ordinary elimination must find usable pivots in order; not every nonsingular matrix admits that exact unpermuted construction. With row pivoting use PA=LU, where P records swaps.
To solve Ax=b, first solve Ly=Pb by forward substitution, then Ux=y by back substitution. The same factors can solve many right-hand sides, avoiding repeated factorization. For dense n×n matrices factorization costs cubic work, while each pair of triangular solves is quadratic. Do not explicitly form an inverse just to solve a single system.
When recording a row operation R₂←R₂−mR₁, L stores m, not −m. If pivoting occurs after earlier multipliers are stored, the corresponding earlier parts of L must be permuted consistently. Always multiply factors to verify the final decomposition and apply P to b as well. Forgetting the right-hand-side permutation gives correct factors but an incorrect solution.
''','Factor A=[[2,1],[4,3]] and solve Ax=(3,7)ᵀ.',r'Factor::L=\begin{bmatrix}1&0\\2&1\end{bmatrix},\quad U=\begin{bmatrix}2&1\\0&1\end{bmatrix}::Multiplier 4/2=2 leaves the second U row (0,1).~Forward substitution::y_1=3,\quad2y_1+y_2=7\Rightarrow y_2=1::Solve Ly=b in increasing row order.~Back substitution::x_2=1,\quad2x_1+x_2=3\Rightarrow x_1=1::Solve Ux=y from the last row upward.', 'L and U as shown; x=(1,1)ᵀ.','LU reconstructs A, and A(1,1)ᵀ=(3,7)ᵀ independently checks the solve.')
T('0-limits-continuity','limits','Limits','Limits and one-sided behavior',r'''
A limit describes the value approached by f(x) when x is near a, without requiring x=a or f(a) to exist. A two-sided limit exists only when left-hand and right-hand limits exist and agree. Direct substitution is valid for continuous expressions at the point; an undefined result such as 0/0 is indeterminate and signals a need to simplify, not an answer of zero.
Factor cancellation, rationalization and standard limits can reveal nearby behavior. Cancel a factor only for x≠a, which is exactly the punctured neighborhood relevant to the limit. For rationalization, multiply by the conjugate and simplify before substituting. Standard trigonometric limits assume radians. When a denominator tends to zero but the numerator does not, sign from each side can yield opposite infinities, so inspect both sides.
For piecewise definitions, evaluate each side using its own branch. Changing a single point’s function value cannot change an existing limit. A GATE option claiming “limit exists, therefore function is defined” confuses nearby values with the point itself; a removable hole provides an immediate counterexample.
''','Evaluate lim x→0 of (√(1+x)−1)/x.',r'Recognize indeterminacy::\frac{\sqrt1-1}{0}=\frac00::Substitution cannot settle the limit.~Rationalize::\frac{\sqrt{1+x}-1}{x}=\frac1{\sqrt{1+x}+1}\quad(x\ne0)::Multiplication by the conjugate cancels x.~Take the limit::\lim_{x\to0}\frac1{\sqrt{1+x}+1}=\frac12::The simplified expression is continuous near zero.', 'The limit is 1/2.','The original expression remains undefined at zero unless a value is explicitly assigned.')
T('0-limits-continuity','continuity','Continuity','Continuity of ordinary and piecewise functions',r'''
Continuity at a requires three things: f(a) is defined, the limit as x→a exists, and that limit equals f(a). Equality of one-sided limits establishes only the second condition. A discontinuity can be removable when a finite common limit exists but the value is missing or different, a jump when the finite sides differ, or infinite when values diverge.
For a piecewise function, distinguish the branch giving f(a) from branches giving approaching values. Set left limit equal to right limit and to the actual assigned value, then solve parameter constraints. At an endpoint of a domain, continuity is interpreted relative to that domain and may require only the available one-sided limit.
Continuity allows important conclusions without asserting differentiability: a continuous function on a closed bounded interval attains a maximum and minimum, and takes intermediate values between endpoint values. A sharp corner can remain continuous. When several continuity conditions involve parameters, solve them simultaneously; satisfying just one transition point does not establish continuity at every junction.
''','Choose c so f(x)=x+1 for x<2 and f(x)=c for x≥2 is continuous at 2.',r'Left limit::\lim_{x\to2^-}(x+1)=3::Use the branch active to the left.~Right value and limit::f(2)=c,\quad\lim_{x\to2^+}f(x)=c::The right branch includes the junction point.~Match::3=c::Both sides and the value must coincide.', 'c=3.','For c=4, f(2) exists but a jump remains; being defined at the point is insufficient.')
T('0-limits-continuity','derivative','Differentiability;Differentiation','Derivatives, rules and differentiability tests',r'''
The derivative is the limit of the difference quotient [f(a+h)−f(a)]/h as h tends to zero. It measures the local linear rate of change. Both one-sided quotients must agree at an interior point. Differentiability implies continuity, but continuity need not imply differentiability: corners, cusps and vertical tangents can prevent a finite derivative.
Power, product, quotient and chain rules avoid recomputing limits for known differentiable forms. Product differentiation includes both varying factors; chain differentiation multiplies by the derivative of the inner expression. For piecewise functions, first enforce continuity at the junction, then compare branch derivatives. Equal formulas for derivative candidates cannot rescue a discontinuous junction because the difference quotient still includes the mismatched point value.
Stationary points satisfy f′=0, but not every stationary point is an extremum and not every extremum has a derivative. The derivative’s sign determines increasing/decreasing intervals where assumptions apply. In GATE, specify whether a question asks for a derivative value, differentiability, or monotonicity; these need different conclusions and often different checks.
''','Differentiate f(x)=x²eˣ.',r'Choose product rule::(uv)^{\prime}=u^{\prime}v+uv^{\prime}::Both x² and eˣ vary.~Differentiate factors::u^{\prime}=2x,\quad v^{\prime}=e^x::Apply power and exponential rules.~Combine::f^{\prime}(x)=2xe^x+x^2e^x=e^x(2x+x^2)::Retain both product-rule contributions.', 'f′(x)=eˣ(2x+x²).','At x=0 the derivative is zero; differentiating only eˣ would incorrectly omit the 2x term elsewhere.')
T('0-extrema-mvt','extrema','Maxima and minima','Local versus absolute extrema and candidate testing',r'''
A local extremum compares a point to nearby points; an absolute extremum compares it to the entire specified domain. A differentiable interior local extremum must have f′=0, but the converse fails: x³ has zero derivative at zero without a maximum or minimum. A nondifferentiable corner such as |x| can still be a minimum.
For absolute extrema of a continuous function on [a,b], collect interior critical points where the derivative is zero or undefined, and include both endpoints. Evaluate f at every candidate and compare values. The extreme-value theorem guarantees attainment on that closed bounded interval, not on arbitrary open or unbounded domains.
For local classification, a derivative sign change from negative to positive proves a minimum; positive to negative proves a maximum. The second-derivative test works when f′=0 and f″ is positive or negative; zero is inconclusive and requires another method. A parameter or domain restriction can remove a candidate, so reject critical roots lying outside the requested interval before comparing values.
''','Classify f(x)=x³ at zero.',r'Stationary test::f^{\prime}(x)=3x^2,\quad f^{\prime}(0)=0::Zero is a stationary candidate.~Second derivative::f^{\prime\prime}(0)=0::This test is inconclusive, not evidence of a minimum.~Compare neighbors::f(-h)<0=f(0)<f(h)\quad(h>0)::Both smaller and larger nearby values exist.', 'Zero is neither a local maximum nor a local minimum.','The derivative is nonnegative on both sides and does not change from increasing to decreasing or vice versa.')
T('0-extrema-mvt','mvt','Mean value theorem','Mean value theorem and its hypotheses',r'''
The mean value theorem states that if f is continuous on [a,b] and differentiable on (a,b), then some interior c has derivative equal to the secant slope [f(b)−f(a)]/(b−a). Geometrically a tangent is parallel to the chord joining endpoints. Rolle’s theorem is the special case f(a)=f(b), giving f′(c)=0.
Check hypotheses before solving the derivative equation. A corner inside the interval can invalidate differentiability even if a secant slope is easy to calculate. The theorem guarantees existence, not uniqueness. A derivative equation can have several eligible roots; include only those strictly inside the interval.
Consequences include monotonicity and bounds: if a derivative is everywhere positive on an interval, the function is strictly increasing there; if |f′|≤M, changes in function value are bounded by M times input distance. Endpoint continuity is needed despite no endpoint differentiability requirement. A GATE problem using an absolute-value function often tests this distinction rather than merely asking to solve f′(c)=a number.
''','Does MVT apply to f(x)=|x| on [−1,1]?',r'Continuity::::Absolute value is continuous on the whole closed interval.~Differentiability::::At zero, left derivative −1 differs from right derivative 1.~Secant slope::\frac{|1|-|-1|}{1-(-1)}=0::No interior differentiable point has derivative zero; MVT hypotheses failed.', 'MVT cannot be applied.','This does not contradict the theorem: its differentiability assumption is missing at zero.')
T('0-integration','integrals','Integration;Definite integrals','Antiderivatives and signed definite integrals',r'''
An antiderivative F satisfies F′=f. The indefinite integral records all such functions as F+C on an interval. A definite integral instead measures accumulated signed contribution across bounds and is a number. Under the fundamental theorem’s conditions, it equals F(b)−F(a); a common constant cancels.
Positive and negative regions contribute opposite signs. Thus the integral is not automatically geometric area, which requires integrating |f| or separating sign intervals. Reversing bounds changes sign, and splitting an interval adds its pieces. Symmetry can simplify: odd integrable functions cancel over [−a,a], while even integrable functions double their contribution over [0,a]. Check integrability before using those rules near singularities.
For improper integrals, replace a singular endpoint or infinite bound by a limit and evaluate convergence. A formally written antiderivative does not make a divergent integral finite. In GATE, track whether the requested quantity is a signed integral, enclosed area, or probability mass; the same expression may require different handling of negative regions.
''','Compare ∫ from −1 to 1 of x dx with geometric area under |x|.',r'Signed integral::\int_{-1}^{1}x\,dx=[x^2/2]_{-1}^1=0::Opposite contributions cancel.~Left absolute area::\int_{-1}^0(-x)\,dx=1/2::Absolute value changes the sign on the negative half.~Right absolute area::\int_0^1x\,dx=1/2::Add the nonnegative areas.', 'Signed integral 0; total geometric area 1.','Odd symmetry explains the signed zero, while two triangles each have area 1/2.')
T('0-integration','techniques','Substitution;Integration by parts','Substitution and integration by parts',r'''
Substitution reverses the chain rule. Choose u=g(x) so du=g′(x)dx replaces the derivative factor, and rewrite the whole integrand in u. For a definite integral transform endpoints as well, or return entirely to x before applying original endpoints; mixing new variables with old bounds is an error. Merely naming u without removing every x does not finish the substitution.
Integration by parts reverses product differentiation: ∫u dv=uv−∫v du. Choose u so differentiation simplifies it and dv so its integral is known. A polynomial times an exponential often simplifies when the polynomial is differentiated; a logarithm often works as u. Include the boundary product for definite integrals.
The techniques can be combined or repeated, but select by structure rather than a memorized label. Verify an indefinite result by differentiating it. In GATE numerical answers, retain exact values until the last step to avoid rounding constants such as e; also inspect whether the bounds make the integrand odd or otherwise symmetric before doing long calculations.
''','Evaluate ∫₀¹ x eˣ dx.',r'Choose parts::u=x,\quad dv=e^x dx,\quad du=dx,\quad v=e^x::Differentiating x simplifies it.~Apply the formula::\int_0^1xe^x dx=[xe^x]_0^1-\int_0^1e^x dx::Keep both definite terms.~Evaluate::e-(e-1)=1::Upper minus lower in the remaining exponential integral.', 'The integral equals 1.','Derivative of (x−1)eˣ is xeˣ, independently confirming the antiderivative.')
T('0-conditional-bayes','conditioning','Conditional probability;Independence','Conditioning, intersections and independence',r'''
Probability assigns normalized weights to events. Conditional probability P(A|B) restricts attention to outcomes in B and renormalizes by P(B), so P(A|B)=P(A∩B)/P(B) when P(B)>0. The multiplication rule P(A∩B)=P(B)P(A|B) does not require independence.
Independence means the intersection probability factors as P(A)P(B), equivalently conditioning on one nonzero-probability event does not change the other’s probability. Disjoint events instead cannot occur together and have zero intersection. Two disjoint events with positive probabilities are therefore dependent, not independent. Draws without replacement usually change remaining probabilities and require conditional factors.
For a chain of events, multiply each probability conditional on earlier events. For unions, use inclusion–exclusion rather than summing overlapping probabilities. Describe the sample space and whether outcomes are equally likely before using favorable/total counts. GATE distractors often invert A|B or reuse the original denominator after a removal; a count-based interpretation of the condition makes both errors visible.
''','A fair die shows an even value. What is the probability it exceeds 3?',r'Conditioned sample space::B=\{2,4,6\}::Only even outcomes remain, each equally likely.~Intersection::A\cap B=\{4,6\}::These outcomes are both even and greater than three.~Normalize::P(A\mid B)=\frac{2/6}{3/6}=\frac23::Divide by the condition’s probability.', '2/3.','Using 3/6 would calculate unconditioned P(value>3), a different question.')
T('0-conditional-bayes','bayes','Bayes theorem','Bayes’ theorem: reverse a conditional using base rates',r'''
Bayes’ theorem derives from calculating an intersection two ways: P(A|B)P(B)=P(B|A)P(A). Reversing a conditional therefore needs prior probabilities, not just swapping letters. With mutually exclusive exhaustive causes Bᵢ, total probability P(A)=ΣP(A|Bᵢ)P(Bᵢ) supplies the denominator.
The posterior for cause Bⱼ is its joint contribution to the observed event divided by every cause’s contribution. In testing, sensitivity P(positive|condition) is not P(condition|positive). Even a good test can produce many false positives when the condition’s prior prevalence is low. A hypothetical population converts percentages into counts and often makes this effect intuitive.
Draw a tree with first branches for prior causes and second branches for evidence likelihoods. Multiply down branches for joint probabilities and sum across evidence-compatible leaves. Only afterward normalize the requested branch. Priors must sum to one and the evidence probability must be nonzero. Keep the full denominator when several machines, boxes or populations can produce the same observed outcome.
''','Machine A makes 60% of items with 2% defects; B makes 40% with 5% defects. Given a defect, find P(B).',r'Joint defect contributions::P(A\cap D)=0.6(0.02)=0.012,\quad P(B\cap D)=0.4(0.05)=0.020::Weight each defect rate by production share.~Total defect probability::P(D)=0.012+0.020=0.032::Either machine can explain the defect.~Posterior::P(B\mid D)=0.020/0.032=5/8::Normalize B’s contribution among defective items.', 'P(B|defect)=0.625.','Despite producing fewer items, B contributes more defects; the posterior need not equal its 40% production share.')
T('0-random-statistics','variables','Random variables;Mean;Standard deviation','Random variables, expectation and spread',r'''
A random variable maps outcomes to numbers; it is not itself a probability. A discrete variable has a probability mass function whose nonnegative masses sum to one. A continuous variable has density integrating to one; interval probability is area, and a single exact value has probability zero in a continuous distribution.
Expectation is the probability-weighted value. It can fall between possible outcomes and obeys linearity without independence. Variance is the expected squared deviation from the mean, equivalently E[X²]−E[X]². Standard deviation is its nonnegative square root, using the original units; variance uses squared units. Adding a constant changes the mean but not variance, while multiplying by a scales variance by a².
For sums, covariance determines whether variances add; independence is a sufficient condition for zero covariance, whereas expectation always adds. In sample statistics distinguish dividing by n for a population variance from n−1 for the conventional unbiased sample estimator. A supplied distribution question normally uses its theoretical expectation/variance, not an estimator convention chosen without explanation.
''','X takes 1 with probability 3/4 and 5 with probability 1/4.',r'Mean::E[X]=1(3/4)+5(1/4)=2::Weight values by probabilities.~Second moment::E[X^2]=1(3/4)+25(1/4)=7::Square outcomes before averaging.~Variance and deviation::Var(X)=7-2^2=3,\quad\sigma=\sqrt3::Subtract the squared mean, then take the root.', 'Mean 2, variance 3, standard deviation √3.','Squared deviations give (1−2)²(3/4)+(5−2)²(1/4)=3, matching the moment calculation.')
T('0-random-statistics','central','Median;Mode','Mean, median and mode summarize different aspects',r'''
For a finite dataset, the arithmetic mean is sum divided by count. The median is a middle location after sorting: for odd count use the central observation, and for even count the usual numerical median averages the two central observations. The mode is a most frequent value; it may be nonunique or absent under a convention requiring repetition.
These statistics need not agree. A large outlier can move the mean substantially while scarcely changing the median. The mode records concentration at a value rather than balancing numerical distances. Sorting is essential for median but does not change mean or frequencies. For a frequency table, weight each value by its count and locate median positions using cumulative counts rather than taking the median of category labels alone.
Combining datasets requires a count-weighted mean; simply averaging two means works only with equal sample sizes. Do not assume symmetry or a normal distribution from one summary statistic. A GATE question may deliberately provide repeated observations or uneven group sizes to test whether each definition, rather than a familiar slogan about an “average,” is being used.
''','Dataset: 1,2,2,5,10,10. Find mean, median and modes.',r'Mean::\overline x=(1+2+2+5+10+10)/6=5::All six observations contribute.~Median::median=(2+5)/2=3.5::The sorted third and fourth positions are central.~Modes::::Both 2 and 10 occur twice, more often than the others.', 'Mean 5; median 3.5; modes 2 and 10.','An even-count median can be a value absent from the data; neither mode must equal the mean.')
T('0-binomial-poisson','binomial','Binomial distribution','Binomial counts from independent fixed trials',r'''
A binomial variable counts successes in n independent Bernoulli trials with common success probability p. Fixed trial count, independence and identical p are all model assumptions. Exactly k successes can occupy C(n,k) subsets of positions. Each ordering has probability pᵏ(1−p)ⁿ⁻ᵏ, and disjoint orderings add to the binomial mass.
The mean np follows by writing the count as a sum of success indicators. Independence makes their variances add, giving np(1−p). At least one success is often easiest as one minus zero successes: 1−(1−p)ⁿ. “At most k” instead requires summing masses from zero through k, not inserting k into the exactly-k formula.
Sampling without replacement is generally not binomial because probabilities and dependence change; use conditional counting or the relevant finite-population model. Degenerate p=0 or p=1 cases deserve interpretation rather than blindly using a formula involving zero powers. A small-case enumeration can verify combinatorial factors and expose counting ordered successes twice.
''','Three independent trials succeed with p=1/4. Find P(exactly one) and P(at least one).',r'One-success arrangements::\binom31=3::Choose its position.~Exactly one::3(1/4)(3/4)^2=27/64::Every other trial must fail.~At least one::1-(3/4)^3=37/64::Subtract the single zero-success event.', 'Exactly one: 27/64. At least one: 37/64.','The second probability must be larger because it also includes two and three successes.')
T('0-binomial-poisson','poisson','Poisson distribution','Poisson counts and converting rate to interval mean',r'''
A Poisson count models events in an interval under a constant-rate process with independent increments and the standard rare-single-event local assumptions. Its parameter λ is the expected count in the whole interval, so rate r over duration t gives λ=rt. The mass is e⁻λ λᵏ/k! for nonnegative integer k, and both mean and variance equal λ.
Probability of zero events is e⁻λ; at least one is its complement. Counts over disjoint independent Poisson intervals add, with parameters adding too. The distribution is unbounded in count even though the time interval is fixed, unlike a binomial variable’s maximum n. A binomial can be approximated by Poisson when n is large, p small and np remains moderate; this is an approximation, not an identity for arbitrary trials.
Keep units consistent before computing λ: a rate per minute cannot multiply a duration in seconds without conversion. Distinguish event count from waiting time, which is exponential under a Poisson process. Standard deviation is √λ, not λ. Those interpretation checks often matter more than factorial arithmetic.
''','Events arrive at 3 per hour. Find probability of no event in 20 minutes.',r'Convert duration::t=20/60=1/3\ hour::Match the rate unit.~Interval mean::\lambda=3(1/3)=1::Expected count in this window is one.~Zero events::P(X=0)=e^{-1}\frac{1^0}{0!}=e^{-1}::Zero factorial and zero exponent each give one.', 'e⁻¹≈0.3679.','Using e⁻³ would describe a full hour, not the requested twenty-minute interval.')
T('0-continuous-distributions','uniform','Uniform distribution','Uniform density and support intersections',r'''
A continuous uniform variable on [a,b] has constant density 1/(b−a) within its support and zero outside. Probability is interval length times density. If a requested interval extends beyond support, intersect it with [a,b] before measuring length. Density has inverse units and can exceed one on a short interval without violating probability bounds.
The cumulative distribution is zero below a, linear within support and one above b. Mean is the midpoint (a+b)/2, and variance is (b−a)²/12. Because single points have probability zero, strict versus inclusive endpoints do not change interval probabilities for this continuous model.
Uniformity means equally long subintervals have equal probability, not that each real-number outcome has some identical positive mass. A discrete uniform choice among integers is different and requires counting points rather than lengths. In GATE, identify which model is stated before converting a range into a numerator; mixing inclusive integer counts with continuous widths produces off-by-one errors.
''','X is uniform on [2,8]. Find P(0<X<5).',r'Intersect support::(0,5)\cap[2,8]=[2,5)::Values below 2 are impossible.~Density::f(x)=1/(8-2)=1/6::Normalize across the six-unit support.~Measure interval::P(0<X<5)=(5-2)/6=1/2::Only the three-unit overlap contributes.', '1/2.','Five lies at the support midpoint, so half the uniform mass lies below it.')
T('0-continuous-distributions','normal','Normal distribution','Normal standardization and symmetry',r'''
A normal variable has bell-shaped density centered at μ with standard deviation σ>0. The parameters specify location and scale; many texts write N(μ,σ²), so read whether the second parameter is variance or standard deviation before calculation. Standardizing Z=(X−μ)/σ produces the standard normal with mean zero and variance one.
Use its cumulative function Φ to express P(X≤x)=Φ((x−μ)/σ), and subtract two CDF values for an interval. Symmetry gives Φ(−z)=1−Φ(z). A continuous probability at exactly the mean is zero even though the density there is largest. Mean, median and mode all equal μ for this distribution, which is a model-specific fact rather than a general rule for every dataset.
When a table is supplied, check whether it reports full left-tail probability or area between zero and z. For a numeric answer without a table, retain the exact Φ expression unless an accepted approximation is specified. Changing variance alters standardized distance by its square root, not by variance itself; GATE distractors commonly divide by the wrong parameter.
''','X~N(10,9), with variance 9. Express P(7≤X≤13).',r'Scale::\sigma=\sqrt9=3::The second parameter is variance.~Standardize bounds::z_1=(7-10)/3=-1,\quad z_2=(13-10)/3=1::Both bounds are one standard deviation from the mean.~CDF difference::P(7\le X\le13)=\Phi(1)-\Phi(-1)=2\Phi(1)-1::Use symmetry.', '2Φ(1)−1, approximately 0.6827.','The interval is μ±σ; dividing by 9 would incorrectly turn it into only one-third of a standard deviation.')
T('0-continuous-distributions','exponential','Exponential distribution','Exponential waiting time and memorylessness',r'''
An exponential variable with rate λ>0 models nonnegative waiting time in a constant-rate Poisson process. Its density is λe⁻λt for t≥0, its survival probability P(T>t)=e⁻λt, mean 1/λ and variance 1/λ². Rate and scale are reciprocal conventions; a parameter described as mean waiting time must not be used as a rate without inversion.
Memorylessness means P(T>s+t|T>s)=P(T>t). Conditioned on already waiting s without an event, the remaining wait has the same distribution. Derive it by dividing e⁻λ(s+t) by e⁻λs. This does not say every real-world waiting process is memoryless; an aging device or fixed appointment schedule may not fit this model.
Interval probabilities subtract survival or CDF values, and probabilities for negative waiting times are zero. Keep λ’s time unit reciprocal to t’s unit. Do not use the Poisson count PMF to answer a waiting-time question; the process relates the two distributions, but one counts events and the other measures elapsed time.
''','Mean wait is 5 minutes. After waiting 3 minutes, find probability of waiting at least 2 more.',r'Convert mean to rate::\lambda=1/5\ per\ minute::The mean is not the rate.~Conditioned survival::P(T>5\mid T>3)=e^{-5/5}/e^{-3/5}::Condition on the already elapsed event-free wait.~Simplify::e^{-1+3/5}=e^{-2/5}::This equals survival for a fresh two-minute wait.', 'e⁻⁰·⁴≈0.6703.','Memorylessness produces the same answer as P(T>2), consistent with the derived conditional ratio.')
